    question: "Explain the difference between object-oriented and object-based programming.",
    slug: "explain-the-difference-between-object-oriented-and-object-based-programming-1",
    categoryName: "Java",
    categorySlug: "java",
    subcategoryName: "Core Java & OOP",
    subcategorySlug: "core-java-oop",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Explain the difference between object-oriented and object-based programming. This answer focuses on the practical reasoning, important trade-offs, and interview points relevant to Core Java & OOP.",
    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain explain the difference between object-oriented and object-based programming. clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in Core Java & OOP.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nObject-based programming uses objects to encapsulate state and behavior but is often used to describe languages or styles that do not provide the full set of class-based object-oriented features. Object-oriented programming is broader and commonly emphasizes encapsulation, abstraction, inheritance, and polymorphism. Java supports these OOP features through classes, interfaces, inheritance, overriding, and dynamic dispatch. JavaScript should not be used as a simple example of a language with no inheritance: it has prototype-based inheritance and polymorphism, even though its object model differs from Java\u2019s class-based model.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nIn Java, you can create a class \"Vehicle\" and extend it with \"Car\" and \"Bike\" subclasses that inherit and override behavior (polymorphism). In an object-based language, you could create a \"Vehicle\" object, but you could not derive a \"Car\" object from it using inheritance \u2014 you'd have to duplicate or manually copy properties.\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Object-oriented programming (OOP) and object-based programming both organize software around objects, but object-oriented programming generally provides a broader set of features, especially inheritance and polymorphism.\n\nObject-based programming focuses on using objects that encapsulate data and behavior. It typically supports concepts such as encapsulation and object identity, but it does not necessarily provide inheritance or runtime polymorphism.\n\n**Object-oriented programming generally combines:**\n\n- Encapsulation: bundling data and the operations that work on that data.\n- Abstraction: exposing essential behavior while hiding implementation details.\n- Inheritance: creating a new class based on an existing class and reusing/extending its behavior.\n- Polymorphism: allowing a common interface to represent different concrete implementations.\n\nFor example, in an object-based design, you might have a BankAccount object containing balance and functions such as deposit() and withdraw(). The object encapsulates its state, but there may be no mechanism for creating specialized account types through inheritance.\n\n**In an object-oriented design, you could have:**\n\n```java\nclass BankAccount {\n    protected double balance;\n\n    void deposit(double amount) {\n        balance += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nclass SavingsAccount extends BankAccount {\n    void applyInterest() {\n        // Savings-specific behavior\n    }\n}\n\n```\nHere SavingsAccount inherits behavior from BankAccount. Polymorphism can then allow code to work with different account implementations through a common BankAccount reference.\n\nThe distinction is not always absolute because terminology varies between languages and textbooks. Java, C++, C#, and similar languages are commonly described as object-oriented because they support classes, inheritance, and polymorphism. JavaScript is object-based/prototype-based but also supports many object-oriented programming techniques. Languages such as Visual Basic historically used the term object-based for systems that supported objects and encapsulation without the full set of classical OOP features.\n\n**A simple comparison is:**\n\nObject-based: objects + encapsulation/object behavior, but inheritance and polymorphism are not necessarily provided.\n\nObject-oriented: objects + encapsulation + abstraction, typically with inheritance and polymorphism.\n\nThe interview takeaway is: object-based programming organizes programs around objects but may lack inheritance and polymorphism, while object-oriented programming provides a more complete object model that supports inheritance and polymorphic behavior.",
