    subcategorySlug: "core-java-oop",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Using Streams, find employee names that start with a particular prefix. This answer focuses on the practical reasoning, important trade-offs, and interview points relevant to Core Java & OOP.",
    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain using streams, find employee names that start with a particular prefix. clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in Core Java & OOP.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThis is a common Stream filtering task. You use the filter() intermediate operation with a predicate that checks String.startsWith(prefix), then map to extract the name field, and collect the results into a List using Collectors.toList(). This pattern demonstrates functional-style data processing instead of traditional for-loops with if-conditions.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nList<Employee> employees = List.of( new Employee(\"Sam\", 50000), new Employee(\"Sara\", 60000), new Employee(\"Tom\", 45000));\nList<String> namesStartingWithSa = employees.stream() .map(Employee::getName) .filter(name -> name.startsWith(\"Sa\")) .collect(Collectors.toList());\n//\nOutput: [Sam, Sara]\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Using the Java Stream API, filter the employees based on the required prefix and then map each matching employee to their name.\n\n**Example employee class:**\n\n```java\nclass Employee {\n    private final String name;\n\n    Employee(String name) {\n        this.name = name;\n    }\n\n    public String getName() {\n        return name;\n    }\n}\n\n```\n**Suppose we have:**\n\n```java\nList<Employee> employees = List.of(\n        new Employee(\"Alice\"),\n        new Employee(\"Bob\"),\n        new Employee(\"Andrew\"),\n        new Employee(\"David\")\n);\n\n```\n**To find names starting with \"A\":**\n\n```java\nList<String> result = employees.stream()\n        .map(Employee::getName)\n        .filter(name -> name.startsWith(\"A\"))\n        .toList();\n\n```\n**The result is:**\n\n[\"Alice\", \"Andrew\"]\n\n**The pipeline works as follows:**\n\n1. employees.stream() creates a stream from the employee list.\n2. map(Employee::getName) converts Employee objects into their names.\n3. filter(name -> name.startsWith(\"A\")) keeps only names with the requested prefix.\n4. toList() collects the matching names into a List.\n\n**If the prefix is supplied dynamically:**\n\n```java\nString prefix = \"An\";\n\nList<String> result = employees.stream()\n        .map(Employee::getName)\n        .filter(name -> name.startsWith(prefix))\n        .toList();\n\n```\nIf null employee names are possible, the pipeline should handle them explicitly:\n\n```java\nList<String> result = employees.stream()\n        .map(Employee::getName)\n        .filter(Objects::nonNull)\n        .filter(name -> name.startsWith(prefix))\n        .toList();\n\nThe operation is generally O(n) because every employee may need to be examined. The prefix comparison itself can depend on the prefix length, although for typical interview discussion the overall operation is described as linear in the number of employees.\n\nThe interview takeaway is: use map() to extract the employee name, filter() with startsWith(prefix) to select matching names, and a terminal operation such as toList() to produce the result.\n```",
