    question: "Why is String immutable in Java?",
    slug: "why-is-string-immutable-in-java-84",
  categoryName: "SQL & Databases",
    categorySlug: "SQL & Databases",
    subcategoryName: "SQL & Databases",
    subcategorySlug: "sql-databases",
    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.EASY,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "Why is String immutable in Java. This answer focuses on the practical reasoning, important trade-offs, and interview points relevant to SQL & Databases.",
    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain why is string immutable in java clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in SQL & Databases.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nString is immutable (its internal character data cannot change after creation) for several important reasons: (1) Security \u2014 strings are often used for sensitive data like file paths, network connections, and class names in classloading, so immutability prevents malicious code from changing a string after it's been validated; (2) String pool optimization \u2014 since strings can't change, the JVM can safely share single instances of identical string literals across the whole application via the string pool, saving memory; (3) Thread-safety \u2014 immutable objects are inherently thread-safe since no thread can modify their state, so multiple threads can share String objects without synchronization; (4) HashMap key reliability \u2014 since String is commonly used as a HashMap key, immutability guarantees its hashCode() never changes after insertion, preventing the key from becoming \"lost\" in the wrong bucket.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nString s = \"hello\";\ns.concat(\" world\");\n// does NOT modify s - concat() returns a NEW String object System.out.println(s);\n// still prints \"hello\" s = s.concat(\" world\");\n// must reassign to capture the\nnew String System.out.println(s);\n// now prints \"hello world\"\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "String is immutable in Java, meaning that once a String object is created, its character content cannot be changed. Operations that appear to modify a String actually create another String object when necessary.\n\nFor example:\n\n```java\nString s = \"Java\";\ns = s + \" Spring\";\n\n```\nThe original \"Java\" String is not modified. A new String representing \"Java Spring\" is created, and the reference s is changed to point to it.\n\nString immutability provides several important benefits.\n\n1. String pool safety\n\nJava can safely share pooled String objects because their contents cannot be changed by one reference and unexpectedly affect another reference.\n\n2. Security\n\nStrings are commonly used for sensitive or security-related values such as class names, file paths, URLs, database connection information, and configuration values. Immutability prevents code that receives a String from modifying the object's content behind the caller's back.\n\n3. Thread safety\n\nImmutable objects can safely be shared between threads without synchronization for their immutable state.\n\n4. Stable hash codes\n\nStrings are frequently used as keys in HashMap and HashSet. Because their content does not change, their hash code remains stable during their lifetime.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\nMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();\nmap.put(\"Java\", 1);\n\n```\nIf the String's contents could later change, locating the entry using the hash table could become problematic.\n\n5. String interning\n\nImmutable Strings can be safely interned and shared through the String pool.\n\n6. Design simplicity\n\nImmutability makes String behavior predictable and avoids synchronization and defensive copying for the character data itself.\n\n```java\nString concatenation in loops can still create many temporary objects, which is why StringBuilder is normally preferred for repeated mutable construction:\n\nStringBuilder builder = new StringBuilder();\nfor (String value : values) {\n    builder.append(value);\n}\nString result = builder.toString();\n\nIt is also important to distinguish an immutable String reference from a final reference. `final` prevents reassignment of a reference; String's immutability means the String object's character content cannot be changed.\n\n```\nInterview takeaway: String immutability enables safe sharing, String pooling, stable hashing, thread-safe use, and predictable behavior. When repeated string construction is required, use StringBuilder rather than repeatedly concatenating Strings.",
