    shortDescription: "What is dependency injection in Spring. This answer focuses on the practical reasoning, important trade-offs, and interview points relevant to Spring Boot & Spring.",
    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain what is dependency injection in spring clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in Spring Boot & Spring.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nIn Spring specifically, dependency injection is implemented via the IoC container (ApplicationContext), which reads bean definitions (from annotations like @Component/@Service/@Repository, or explicit @Bean methods in @Configuration classes) and automatically wires dependencies between beans \u2014 resolving which implementation to inject based on type (and qualifiers/names when multiple candidates exist) \u2014 via constructor injection, setter injection, or field injection using @Autowired.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Repository class UserRepository {\n    /* data access logic */\n}\n@Service class UserService {\n    private final UserRepository userRepository;\n    @Autowired\n    // Spring resolves and injects the UserRepository bean automatically\n    public UserService(UserRepository userRepository) {\n        this.userRepository = userRepository;\n    }\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "Dependency Injection (DI) in Spring is the process of having the Spring IoC container create and provide the objects that a class depends on instead of the class creating those dependencies itself.\n\n**Without dependency injection:**\n\n```java\nclass OrderService {\n    private PaymentService paymentService = new PaymentService();\n}\n\n```\nOrderService is tightly coupled to the concrete construction of PaymentService.\n\n**With Spring DI:**\n\n@Service\n```java\nclass PaymentService {\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentService paymentService;\n\n    public OrderService(PaymentService paymentService) {\n        this.paymentService = paymentService;\n    }\n}\n\n```\nSpring detects these components, creates their beans, resolves the dependency, and supplies PaymentService to OrderService.\n\nThe Spring IoC container manages bean creation, dependency wiring, lifecycle, and scope.\n\n**DI is especially useful with interfaces:**\n\n```java\npublic interface PaymentGateway {\n    void charge(BigDecimal amount);\n}\n\n@Component\nclass StripePaymentGateway implements PaymentGateway {\n    public void charge(BigDecimal amount) {\n        // implementation\n    }\n}\n\n@Service\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway paymentGateway;\n\n    public OrderService(PaymentGateway paymentGateway) {\n        this.paymentGateway = paymentGateway;\n    }\n}\n\n```\nIf multiple PaymentGateway implementations exist, Spring can use @Primary, @Qualifier, or explicit configuration to select the appropriate bean.\n\nFor example:\n\n@Service\n```java\nclass OrderService {\n    private final PaymentGateway gateway;\n\n    OrderService(@Qualifier(\"stripePaymentGateway\") PaymentGateway gateway) {\n        this.gateway = gateway;\n    }\n}\n\n```\n**DI provides several benefits:**\n\n- Loose coupling.\n- Easier unit testing.\n- Easier replacement of implementations.\n- Clear dependency boundaries.\n- Better separation of responsibilities.\n- Centralized object configuration.\n\n**Constructor injection is generally preferred for required dependencies:**\n\n```java\npublic OrderService(PaymentGateway gateway) {\n    this.gateway = gateway;\n}\n\n```\nIt makes dependencies explicit and allows fields to be final.\n\nSpring supports other injection mechanisms, including setter injection and field injection, but constructor injection is usually the cleanest default for required dependencies.\n\nInterview takeaway: Spring DI means the IoC container creates and supplies dependencies to application objects. It reduces coupling, improves testability, and centralizes object wiring and lifecycle management.",
    keyPoints: [
      "In Spring specifically, dependency injection is implemented via the IoC container (ApplicationContext), which reads bean definitions (from annotations like...",
      "Separate framework behavior from application-specific configuration and defaults."
    ],
