    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain lazy vs eager fetching clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in Hibernate & JPA.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThese are fetching strategies that control WHEN related/associated entities are loaded from the database relative to the owning entity. LAZY fetching defers loading the associated entity/collection until it's actually accessed in code (e.g., calling a getter for the first time triggers an additional query) \u2014 this improves initial query performance by avoiding unnecessary data loading, but can cause a LazyInitializationException if accessed after the session/persistence context has closed. EAGER fetching loads the associated entity/collection immediately, as part of the initial query (often via a JOIN), guaranteeing the data is always available even outside the original session, but at the cost of potentially loading much more data than needed, especially for large collections, and contributing to over-fetching or the N+1 problem if not managed carefully. JPA defaults: @ManyToOne and @OneToOne are EAGER by default; @OneToMany and @ManyToMany are LAZY by default.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n@Entity class Department {\n    @OneToMany(mappedBy = \"department\", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\n    // default\n    for collections private List<Employee> employees;\n    // not loaded from DB until .getEmployees() is actually called\n    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)\n    // default\n    for single associations private Company company;\n    // loaded immediately along with the Department itself\n}\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "LAZY and EAGER define when an associated entity or collection should be loaded from the database.\n\nLAZY fetching means the related data is loaded only when it is actually accessed. EAGER fetching means the provider is required to make the association available immediately when the owning entity is loaded, although the exact SQL strategy used to achieve that can vary.\n\n**Example:**\n\n@OneToMany(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)\n```java\nprivate List<Order> orders;\n\nWith LAZY fetching, loading a Customer does not necessarily load all of its orders immediately. Hibernate can load the orders when customer.getOrders() is accessed while the persistence context is available.\n\n```\n**With EAGER:**\n\n@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.EAGER)\nprivate Department department;\n\nThe associated Department is expected to be available when the Employee is loaded.\n\nLAZY is generally preferred for collections and relationships that are not always needed because it avoids unnecessary database work, memory usage, and large object graphs.\n\nEAGER can be convenient when the relationship is small and always required, but using it broadly can create unexpected joins, additional queries, large result sets, and performance problems.\n\nJPA defaults are also important in interviews: @ManyToOne and @OneToOne default to EAGER, while @OneToMany and @ManyToMany default to LAZY. In production code, explicitly specifying fetch behavior is often clearer.\n\nHowever, LAZY does not automatically mean one SQL query only. Hibernate may still issue additional SQL when lazy associations are accessed. Therefore, fetch strategy should be designed according to the use case rather than simply changing everything to LAZY or EAGER.\n\nFor example, a Customer list API may need only customer information, so orders should remain LAZY. A customer-details API that needs orders can explicitly fetch them using JOIN FETCH, EntityGraph, or another appropriate query strategy.\n\nA good production rule is: keep associations LAZY by default where practical, then explicitly fetch the data required by each use case.",
    keyPoints: [
      "These are fetching strategies that control WHEN related/associated entities are loaded from the database relative to the owning entity.",
      "LAZY fetching defers loading the associated entity/collection until it's actually accessed in code (e.g., calling a getter for the first time triggers an additional query) \u2014 this...",
      "EAGER fetching loads the associated entity/collection immediately, as part of the initial query (often via a JOIN), guaranteeing the data is always available even outside the original...",
      "JPA defaults: @ManyToOne and @OneToOne are EAGER by default; @OneToMany and @ManyToMany are LAZY by default."
    ],
