    experienceLevel: ExperienceLevel.FRESHER,
    difficulty: Difficulty.MEDIUM,
    interviewType: InterviewType.TECHNICAL,
    shortDescription: "How would you investigate a slow Spring Boot API. This answer focuses on the practical reasoning, important trade-offs, and interview points relevant to Spring Boot & Spring.",
    explanation: "This question checks whether the candidate can explain how would you investigate a slow spring boot api clearly and connect the answer to practical Java development. The source answer is kept as the basis for the interview response, with emphasis on the behavior, trade-offs, and edge cases that matter in Spring Boot & Spring.",
    sampleAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nA systematic approach: (1) Check APM/tracing tools (New Relic, Datadog, Zipkin) to identify which layer (controller, service, database, external call) is consuming the most time; (2) Enable and inspect SQL logs / Hibernate statistics to spot slow or excessive queries (particularly N+1 problems); (3) Use a profiler (JFR, async-profiler) to capture a CPU/allocation profile under load and identify hot methods; (4) Check for blocking I/O calls to external services without timeouts, which can tie up threads; (5) Review thread pool and connection pool configuration/metrics (via Actuator) for exhaustion or contention; (6) Load test with tools like JMeter or Gatling to reproduce the slowdown in a controlled environment and correlate metrics; (7) Check for unnecessary serialization/deserialization overhead or overly large response payloads.\n\n**Example:**\n\n```java\n// Adding timing instrumentation via an AOP aspect to quickly pinpoint slow methods\n@Around(\"execution(* com.example.service.*.*(..))\") public Object timeIt(ProceedingJoinPoint pjp) throws Throwable {\n    long start = System.nanoTime();\n    Object result = pjp.proceed();\n    long durationMs = (System.nanoTime() - start) / 1_000_000;\n    if (durationMs > 500) {\n        // flag anything over 500ms log.warn(\"{\n    }\n    took {\n    }\n    ms\", pjp.getSignature(), durationMs); } return result; }\n```",
    detailedAnswer: "I would approach a slow Spring Boot API as a measurement and isolation problem rather than immediately changing code. The goal is to identify where the latency is actually being spent.\n\nFirst, I would establish the baseline: endpoint, request volume, p50/p95/p99 latency, error rate, payload size, and whether the problem affects all requests or only particular inputs.\n\nNext, I would trace a slow request through the application. Distributed tracing or APM can show time spent in controller logic, service methods, database calls, external HTTP calls, messaging, and other dependencies.\n\nFor database-related latency, I would inspect SQL queries, query execution plans using EXPLAIN, indexes, connection-pool wait time, lock contention, and N+1 query patterns. A common mistake is optimizing Java code when the actual bottleneck is a database query.\n\nFor external services, I would check connection time, DNS/TLS overhead, remote latency, retries, timeout configuration, and whether calls are being made sequentially when they could safely be parallelized.\n\nFor JVM problems, I would inspect CPU, heap usage, GC pauses, thread states, allocation rate, and thread-pool saturation. Java Flight Recorder, JDK Mission Control, metrics, and thread dumps are useful here.\n\nI would also check HikariCP metrics. If threads are waiting for database connections, increasing application threads will not solve the underlying bottleneck and may make it worse.\n\nAfter identifying the bottleneck, I would apply a targeted fix such as:\n- Adding or correcting a database index.\n- Eliminating N+1 queries.\n- Reducing unnecessary data retrieval.\n- Adding an appropriate cache.\n- Batching database operations.\n- Optimizing serialization or large payloads.\n- Configuring HTTP client connection pools and timeouts.\n- Fixing thread-pool saturation.\n- Moving long-running work to asynchronous processing.\n\nFinally, I would load-test the change and compare p50/p95/p99 latency, throughput, error rate, CPU, memory, DB load, and connection-pool metrics.\n\nA strong production approach is: observe -> trace -> isolate -> measure -> change one major thing -> load test -> monitor the result.",
